L wrote:

> (Another) one of the ideas from #nethack ideas collection #7 gets
> implemented - this one makes it so that under a certain condition your
> character is "innumerate" - they cannot count the quantities of items.
> The numerical values for stacks of objects ("12 appples") are replaced
> with quantifying adjectives ("several apples").
> 
> Currently, the criteria for innumeracy is "Int < 8". I have some
> reservations about this, but I can't think of anything more
> appropriate.
> 
Interesting idea. It would be even more fun it it wasn't so accurate, how 
about:

      if (ACURR(A_INT) < 8 && quan > 3L) {
          quan += rn2(quan);
          if (quan < 9L) return "some";
          if (quan < 21L) return "several";
          if (quan < 30L) return "many";
          if (quan < 300L) return "lots of";
          if (quan < 3000L) return "hundreds of";
          return "thousands of";
      }

i.e. add on a random number in the range 0..quan-1 and increase each of the 
ranges by 50%. That would give some for 4..9, several for 5..20, many for 
11..29 and so on instead of 4..5, 6..12, 13..20 respectively. Your 12 
apples would still usually be several, but could occasionally be many 
instead.

Paying someone while innumerate should result (with high probability) in 
your overpaying them. After all you are trusting the shopkeeper (or 
whoever) to count for you.


